            <div xml:id="d618">

                <pb facs="0561" n="554" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg554"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/10274: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-27T19:00:00+09:00">September 27, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 27—9:01 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>423. Our 421, September 27, 5 p.m. As indicative of one of the considerations
                    entering into the decision to cease bombing of Nanking, we are informed by an
                    entirely reliable Japanese source that the Foreign Office has been “deluged”
                    with telegrams from Japanese diplomatic missions in various parts of the world,
                    including the United States, reporting on movements to boycott Japanese
                    goods.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
